A||N||2||Dean||Runs rough when cold||||1||
Z||000000||Dean||11-28-2001||08:58 AM||||I have an 86 4 cylinder that runs really rough when started, and runs rough and misses until up to operating temperature. I've just checked the timing and set it to the specified 8 degrees. Other basics like fuel supply, plug, wires, etc, and such have been checked. I don't know what mechanism is responsible for cold start and operation until warm, but I suspect that is the culprit. Compression builds quickly to 180, and is consistent on all cylinders.<P>The computer does not indicate any fault codes when checked. The engine just runs rough when cold, and in florida that's not very cold!<P>Any help appreciated. Private replies accepted also.<P>Dean<BR>Dean_Palmer@jabil.com<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||deceler8||11-28-2001||09:09 AM||||In Florida I'd suspect a moisture-related cause, like plug wires gone bad or water in the distributor cap.<P>The air preheat system on the air cleaner assembly could cause this, as could a faulty thermostat or coolant temp sensor.<P>Vacuum leaks are a possibility as well.||||reg||1
Z||000002||84Bill||11-28-2001||01:00 PM||||On rare occasions the ignition module will act up until it gets warm. The ignition mod will not throw codes when it goes bad and it's usually it's the other way around, runs ok until warmed up. It can be a pricey expariment but worth a look.<P>Another thing to look for is a (small) vaccume leak. It may be small enough for the ECM to compensate for and just within limits for it not to thow a lean code.<P>Just an FYI on cold start and run. <BR>The ECM is in open loop operation until the engine warms up. <BR>When in closed loop the ECM takes information from the sensors and adjusts the air/fuel mixture. <BR>In open loop the ECM sets air/fuel tward the rich side and ignores sensor inputs.<P><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 11-28-2001).]||||reg||1
